usb: typec: ucsi: Try a timed-out init again

On a Dell XPS 14 DA14260 the PPM now and then stops answering partway
through init at boot, and ucsi_init_work() gives up on anything but
-EPROBE_DEFER, so the machine has no Type-C ports or charger reports
until the next boot. Binding the driver again recovers it. A failed
ucsi_init() has already unregistered the ports and reset the PPM, which
is what binding again starts with, so start it again, up to three times
a second apart. Its last step fails without unwinding, so an init that
left its connectors registered isn't started again.

--- a/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c
@@ -2230,6 +2230,9 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(ucsi_resume);
 
+/* Fresh inits to try after one times out, a second apart. */
+#define UCSI_INIT_TIMEOUT_RETRIES	3
+
 static void ucsi_init_work(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct ucsi *ucsi = container_of(work, struct ucsi, work.work);
@@ -2239,6 +2242,22 @@
 	if (ret)
 		dev_err_probe(ucsi->dev, ret, "PPM init failed\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * A PPM that stops answering partway through init has been seen to
+	 * answer a fresh init: ucsi_init() unregistered the ports and reset
+	 * the PPM on the way out, so starting it again is the same as
+	 * binding the driver again. Without this a timeout leaves the
+	 * machine with no Type-C ports until the next boot. Its last step
+	 * fails without unwinding, leaving the connectors registered, so
+	 * only an init that left none behind is started again.
+	 */
+	if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT && !ucsi->connector &&
+	    ucsi->work_count++ < UCSI_INIT_TIMEOUT_RETRIES) {
+		dev_warn(ucsi->dev, "PPM init timed out, trying again\n");
+		queue_delayed_work(system_long_wq, &ucsi->work, HZ);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (ret == -EPROBE_DEFER) {
 		if (ucsi->work_count++ > UCSI_ROLE_SWITCH_WAIT_COUNT) {
 			dev_err(ucsi->dev, "PPM init failed, stop trying\n");
